<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      The silo host's own unit suite. ADR-018 — the sibling test project — which
      Directory.Build.targets already grants InternalsVisibleTo by name, and which until now did not
      exist, so that grant was inert.

      ⚠ WHAT IT IS FOR, IN ONE SENTENCE: LocalKubeconfigFiles is the one file in this host that is
      ordinary code rather than composition — it parses a credential reference, decides whether the
      path it names is inside a directory this silo was given, and reads a file — and it was the
      largest single row behind CyberCloud.Silo.Host's 32.5 % on the coverage ratchet.

      ⚠ COVERAGE IS THE SYMPTOM AND NOT THE SUBJECT. What is asserted here is the pair of properties
      the file exists for: the resolver is OPT-IN, so a silo given no root keeps
      KubeApiClientFactory's refusal rather than reading anything; and it is ROOTED, so a reference
      that resolves outside the configured directory is refused BEFORE the filesystem is touched.
      The second half is the one with teeth — a ClusterConnectionDescriptor is written by a
      reconciler, and a rooted path check that is not one turns a provider bug into read access to
      the whole host.

      What this suite does NOT do: build a silo. The opt-in half is a claim about a composed host, so
      it is asserted in CyberCloud.Hosts.Tests against the real SiloComposition.BuildAsync, next to
      every other claim about what these two hosts compose. Nothing here starts a process, a
      container or a cluster.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Silo.Host/CyberCloud.Silo.Host.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
